    "topic_code": "1.12",
    "topic_title": "Confirming Continuity over an Interval",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = ((x−1)√(x+1))/((x−1)(x−2)). What is the largest interval containing x = 0 on which f is continuous? Use one-sided continuity at any included endpoint.",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "[−1, 2)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Cancellation erases a domain restriction: canceling x−1 does not define the original function at x = 1. This interval contains that undefined point, so f is not continuous throughout it. Fix: Record excluded denominator zeros before canceling factors."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "[−1, 1)",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The square root requires x ≥ −1, and the denominator excludes x = 1 and x = 2. The function is continuous throughout [−1, 1), including right-hand continuity at −1; extending this interval would include an undefined point."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "(−1, 1)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Endpoint exclusion error: this interval works, but it is not the largest. Since f(−1) = 0 and the right-hand limit at −1 is 0, the left endpoint can be included. Fix: Check the endpoint value against the appropriate one-sided limit."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "[−1, 1]",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Finite-limit misconception: a finite left-hand limit at x = 1 does not make that endpoint continuous. The original formula leaves f(1) undefined. Fix: Verify that every included endpoint has a defined function value."
     }
    ]
   },
